#{[{:activity/id #uuid "00c1cd54-a7af-5240-860b-4432978fec0c" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097663 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097721 :tutorial/content "Suppose $g$ is differentiable at $a$ and $f$ is differentiable at $b=g(a)$. We prove\n$$ (f\\circ g)'(a)=f'(g(a))g'(a). $$\nDifferentiability of $f$ at $b$ lets us write\n$$ f(b+h)-f(b)=f'(b)h+h\\varepsilon(h), $$\nwhere $\\varepsilon(h)\\to0$ as $h\\to0$; set $\\varepsilon(0)=0$. This identity also holds when $h=0$.\n\nTake $h=g(x)-g(a)$. For $x\\ne a$,\n$$\n\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{x-a}\n=\\left[f'(g(a))+\\varepsilon(g(x)-g(a))\\right]\n\\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a}.\n$$\nSince differentiability implies continuity, $g(x)-g(a)\\to0$ as $x\\to a$. The bracket tends to $f'(g(a))$, and the remaining quotient tends to $g'(a)$. Their product therefore tends to $f'(g(a))g'(a)$, proving the rule.\n\nThis argument does not divide by $g(x)-g(a)$, which may be zero arbitrarily close to $a$." :tutorial/id #uuid "d34fc877-5572-53a2-b957-2c5d44a488f3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13330 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Chain Rule"} :step/expected-seconds 75.0 :step/id #uuid "9ad2def5-511c-587f-9847-fa7af8e06405" :step/math-academy-id 466057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097696 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097785 :tutorial/content "The **chain rule** for differentiation states that the derivative of a composite function $f(g(x))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nThere is some intuition behind this rule: if we think of the derivatives as fractions, the ${\\textrm{d}g}$'s would appear to cancel out.\n\n$$\n\\frac{df}{dx} = \\frac{df}{dg} \\cdot \\frac{dg}{dx}\n$$\n\nTo see how the chain rule can be used to differentiate a function like\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1+2x)^3\n$$\n\nfirst, notice that the function can be written as a composite function\n\n$$\nf(g) = g^3, \\qquad g(x) = 1+2x\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\frac{df}{dg} \\cdot \\frac{dg}{dx} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dg}(g^{3}) \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(1 + 2x) \\\\\n&= 3g^{2} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 6g^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\ng(x) = 1+2x\n$$\n\nfor $g$, we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= 6(1 + 2x)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll provide a derivation of the chain rule at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "743ad997-8732-5c0f-9676-ff1d44f3b513" :tutorial/math-academy-id 415 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/expected-seconds 135.0 :step/id #uuid "a2d483f7-5abb-5070-9d8d-2db12d3f0358" :step/math-academy-id 773 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097697}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096760 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096323} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050628}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244582}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"} :step/expected-seconds 195.0 :step/id #uuid "7f46eb68-796d-5c0d-a89b-b1dbdf45d975" :step/math-academy-id 774 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097698}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097698 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096761 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096324} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f0cdac9-57b2-5634-8d98-d6adf8a85204" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247756}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"} :step/expected-seconds 195.0 :step/id #uuid "60f88dd0-c1bd-546f-9dac-f440401fd02b" :step/math-academy-id 777 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097699}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097699 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097786 :tutorial/content "The chain rule can also be expressed using primes instead of Leibniz notation.\n\nThe chain rule, in prime notation, states that\n\n$$\n(f(g(x)))' = f'(g(x)) \\cdot g'(x)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "58c36c82-b739-55f9-8e99-4df1e0cbde24" :tutorial/math-academy-id 418 :tutorial/title "Another Notation for the Chain Rule"} :step/expected-seconds 30.0 :step/id #uuid "90637184-1911-5cfb-bf2c-dbdad69086a3" :step/math-academy-id 780 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097700}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097700 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096762 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096325} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247763}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"} :step/expected-seconds 240.0 :step/id #uuid "35489675-a0a1-59ea-b1f7-ab794d855f54" :step/math-academy-id 781 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097663}}] :activity/title "The Chain Rule for Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson}}]}
